# Session Summary — March 12, 2026

## What we did

Designed and built mobile versions of all three core UI surfaces: Chat Selector, Settings, and Conversation (Chat Interface). Iterated through multiple rounds on each, arriving at a mobile-specific interaction model branded **Swift — Mobile** (fast, agile, compact). The conversation UI diverged significantly from a direct desktop port into its own gesture-driven paradigm.

## Mockup Files Created

| File | Description |
|------|-------------|
| `chat-selector-mobile.jsx` | Mobile chat selector — flat list with bottom tab bar, swipe-right selection mode, floating bulk action bar |
| `settings-mobile.jsx` | Mobile settings — accordion pattern, all 7 sections, bottom sheets for modals |
| `chat-interface-mobile.jsx` | **Swift mode** conversation UI — gesture-driven, unified panel, tap-to-select cards, context-health-colored controls |

## Swift — Mobile: Design Philosophy

The mobile conversation UI is not a responsive port of the desktop — it's a distinct interaction model optimized for one-handed touch use. The name "Swift" reflects three principles:

- **Fast:** Reduced interaction steps. Swipe gestures replace multi-tap navigation. Tap-to-select replaces three-dot menus. Context health is ambient, not a separate panel.
- **Agile:** A single unified panel replaces two separate sidebars. Expandable sections let users jump between tools without opening and closing different drawers.
- **Compact:** The top bar is a single icon. Message cards have no header chrome. The send button doubles as a context health indicator. Every pixel serves a purpose.

